WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.220
Two neurons share one target.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.400
One starts with a tiny lead.

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.220
Does the lead grow, shrink, or stay put?

4
00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:15.583
Neuron A carries a little more TrkA, the receptor that binds NGF.

5
00:00:16.633 --> 00:00:19.333
TrkA is a receptor tyrosine kinase.

6
00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:23.213
More receptor means the neuron catches more of the supply.

7
00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:27.397
By chance, A catches 52 percent of the NGF.

8
00:00:28.477 --> 00:00:29.657
Neuron B catches the rest.

9
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.730
Now add feedback on receptor level.

10
00:00:33.670 --> 00:00:37.370
Each neuron makes TrkA in proportion to the NGF it catches.

11
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.980
And each neuron's catch is simply proportional to its TrkA.

12
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:45.103
Both neurons now make more receptor.

13
00:00:45.923 --> 00:00:48.003
But B grows by the same fraction as A.

14
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:51.797
After many rounds, what share does neuron A hold?

15
00:00:58.217 --> 00:01:04.077
Still 52 Proportional sharing keeps the ratio fixed, round after round.

16
00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:08.687
Each neuron's gain is a fixed fraction of what it already has.

17
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.130
Add some steady basal synthesis of TrkA.

18
00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:16.810
Both neurons get the same top-up, so the shares drift back toward even.

19
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:22.133
So in this setup, feedback on receptor level alone does not make a winner.

20
00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:25.627
Now let NGF do two things at once.

21
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:28.907
It raises TrkA, so the neuron catches more.

22
00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.160
It also lengthens each signal, so every caught molecule counts for longer.

23
00:01:35.233 --> 00:01:39.513
For simple arithmetic, assume each signal lasts in proportion to the catch.

24
00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:43.033
And TrkA is made in proportion to the signal.

25
00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:46.133
Then each round squares the ratio of A to B.

26
00:01:48.217 --> 00:01:52.757
Watch A's share climb, round after round, until it holds almost all of it.

27
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.320
The lead feeds itself.

28
00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.540
That is positive feedback, the loop you met in signaling circuits.

29
00:02:05.533 --> 00:02:07.433
Here is the condition it needs.

30
00:02:08.273 --> 00:02:11.693
A lead can grow only when the reward rises faster than the lead.

31
00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:15.043
What about arriving first?

32
00:02:15.983 --> 00:02:18.523
Arriving first may well give a neuron its small lead.

33
00:02:19.517 --> 00:02:24.577
Arrival order alone cannot explain how a small lead grows while the supply keeps

34
00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:25.577
coming.

35
00:02:28.217 --> 00:02:33.077
And the target keeps releasing NGF throughout the death period, not just at first.

36
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:37.917
The simulation in the lesson splits its neurons another way.

37
00:02:38.597 --> 00:02:41.137
Its TrkA response to each neuron's share is switch-like.

38
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:48.340
Takeaway. For identical neurons with proportional sharing and no basal synthesis, a

39
00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:49.420
lead stays frozen.

40
00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.060
To grow, the reward has to rise faster than the lead.
